From a16e285924cd76d98a2a4f32ad2a53b256401818 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: william arzt <bill@tlon.io>
Date: Fri, 27 Nov 2026 15:14:53 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] urwasm: keep-alive: interrupts on other threads, memory
 pressure, sizing

Fixes from a fifth review round.

- A signal sent to the process while the event thread holds signals off
  could be delivered to another thread (the worker has libuv's pool), whose
  handler would then jump to u3_Signal on the event thread's stack. The
  handlers now pass such a signal to the event thread. (Windows already
  does the same in rsignal_longjmp.)
- Emptying a slot forgets a mapping only after unmapping it, so that a
  stack overflow in the middle leaves it for u3we_lia_reap() to finish.
- When the system will not back more of a reservation, idle instances are
  dropped and the request retried before the event fails.
- The budget counts memory and code as far as they were made usable, not
  as far as they are in use.
- Nothing makes way for a new instance until it has been sized: there is
  one spare slot to build in, and _uw_hot_fit() evicts only if that is
  enough, before any of its memory is taken.
- u3we_lia_wipe() drops ready instances wherever the persistent memo cache
  is emptied.
- The saved struct image is no longer zeroed before it is overwritten.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.2 <noreply@anthropic.com>
